{
  "id": "binary-tree-inorder-traversal",
  "questionId": "binary-tree-inorder-traversal",
  "slug": "binary-tree-inorder-traversal",
  "title": "Binary Tree Inorder Traversal",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "recursion",
    "traversal"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "inorder means: everything on the left first, then me, then everything on the right. what recursive call order does that suggest?",
    "write a helper dfs(i, result) that appends to a shared result list, given the current node index",
    "the base case is when the index is out of bounds or holds -101 - just return without appending anything",
    "the recursive case is: dfs(2*i+1) first, then append tree[i], then dfs(2*i+2)"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "visiting the node before recursing into the left subtree, which produces preorder instead of inorder",
    "forgetting to recurse into the right subtree at all",
    "not checking the -101 sentinel in the base case, only the array bounds, causing sentinel values to leak into the output"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees are encoded as flat arrays using complete-binary-tree (heap) indexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array's bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`, which means \"no node there\"\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2's left child is missing and its right child is 4.\n\n**Return the inorder traversal of the tree, as a plain list: visit the entire left subtree, then the current node, then the entire right subtree, applied recursively.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= number of real nodes <= 1000",
      "every real node value is between -100 and 100 inclusive",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "empty tree, which returns an empty array",
      "single node tree, which returns a single-element array",
      "a tree that is entirely a left chain or entirely a right chain"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [5, 4, 8, 11, 2, -101, 1]",
        "output": "[11, 4, 2, 5, 8, 1]",
        "expectedOutput": "[11, 4, 2, 5, 8, 1]",
        "explanation": "root 5 has left child 4 (with children 11, 2) and right child 8 (with only a right child 1). inorder visits 4's subtree (11, 4, 2), then 5, then 8's subtree (8, 1, since 8 has no left child)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, -101, 2, -101, -101, 3, -101]",
        "output": "[1, 3, 2]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 3, 2]",
        "explanation": "root 1 has no left child, only right child 2; 2's left child is 3. inorder visits 1 (no left subtree), then 2's subtree (3, then 2)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [-101]",
        "output": "[]",
        "expectedOutput": "[]",
        "explanation": "an empty tree produces an empty traversal"
      }
    ],
